          "secNumber": "6.1",
          "heading": "Foundational Principles & Theory: Gas Absorption and Stripping in Staged Columns",
          "content": "## Why this unit matters\n\nAbsorption transfers a solute from gas to a liquid solvent; stripping reverses the direction, typically by contacting a loaded liquid with vapor or gas. Their performance depends on phase equilibrium, mass-transfer rates, solvent loading, temperature, pressure, and approach to pinch.\n\nA solvent that produces excellent equilibrium capacity may perform poorly if viscous, corrosive, volatile, expensive, difficult to regenerate, or incompatible with the downstream process. Solvent regeneration energy often dominates the operating cost.\n\n## Four lessons\n\n- [Lesson 6.1: Solvent selection](../lessons/u06-l01.html)\n- [Lesson 6.2: Equilibrium stages for absorption](../lessons/u06-l02.html)\n- [Lesson 6.3: Transfer-unit methods](../lessons/u06-l03.html)\n- [Lesson 6.4: Solvent regeneration and stripping](../lessons/u06-l04.html)\n\n## Integration and modeling challenge\n\nBuild a balance diagram for a process in this unit, list the specified and unknown variables, and decide whether equilibrium, capacity, mass-transfer rate or dynamics limits the result. Solve a base case and two independently chosen disturbances. Include a unit table and one measured or reference property that could falsify the model. Record the critical operating constraint that would prevent an unreviewed classroom design from being built.\n\n## Engineering review prompts\n\n- Which performance variables represent product quality, recovery, consumption and safety, and which are merely convenient intermediate calculations?\n- What information is missing from the simplified analysis that would be needed in a hazard review or engineering procurement document?\n- Can an alternative separation configuration lower energy consumption at the cost of higher solvent makeup, pressure drop or maintenance?\n- If a separator meets equilibrium purity in a diagram but not in operation, what measurements discriminate insufficient stages from hydraulic or analyzer problems?\n\n## Instructor note\n\nSuccessful work shows traceable calculations and clearly communicates the validity of assumptions. A complete case study should be reproducible from the given data and should not hide convergence failures or infeasible conditions. If a proposed design is infeasible, recognizing and explaining that infeasibility is a technically correct outcome."
