          "id": "sec-6-2",
          "secNumber": "6.2",
          "heading": "Process Simulation & Industrial Design Case: Gas Absorption and Stripping in Staged Columns",
          "content": "# Applied project 6: Absorption and stripping\n\n## Deliverable\n\nPrepare a ten-page preliminary engineering note for a separation process with a 100 kmol/h feed and at least two specified product qualities. You may change the illustrative feed quantities where the selected operation requires a solvent or additional phase. Present a process flow diagram, component balance table, energy or solvent-use estimate, model comparison, sensitivity plot and recommendation with a clearly labeled uncertainty range.\n\n## Process-specific work\n\nAbsorption transfers a solute from gas to a liquid solvent; stripping reverses the direction, typically by contacting a loaded liquid with vapor or gas. Their performance depends on phase equilibrium, mass-transfer rates, solvent loading, temperature, pressure, and approach to pinch.\n\nA solvent that produces excellent equilibrium capacity may perform poorly if viscous, corrosive, volatile, expensive, difficult to regenerate, or incompatible with the downstream process. Solvent regeneration energy often dominates the operating cost.\n\nThe scope of the report must include all four topics in this unit: Solvent selection, Equilibrium stages for absorption, Transfer-unit methods, Solvent regeneration and stripping. The first analysis should be an analytic or graphical approximation. The second should be an independently formulated balance or numeric calculation. These cannot simply be two screenshots of the same routine.\n\n## Scenarios\n\n**Design point:** Adopt a declared separation target and calculate the minimum feasible separation under the selected assumptions. **High-load case:** Increase the feed throughput by 30% and examine hydraulic or solvent constraints. **Feed-quality disturbance:** Change the critical component feed fraction by 10 percentage points, and verify that all product streams can remain nonnegative. **Utility disturbance:** Reduce the available heat or solvent by 20% and determine whether the target remains feasible. Do not extrapolate the simplified model through a phase transition or into a known invalid operating region.\n\n## Acceptance checklist\n\nA satisfactory report identifies one physically infeasible case, includes closure of every component balance, presents at least two plausible competing designs, and explains the major limitation of the selected model. Include one risk associated with equipment safety and one risk involving waste or utility use. The result is a classroom design exercise, not a code-compliant plant design."
