          "id": "sec-6-3",
          "secNumber": "6.3",
          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: Gas Absorption and Stripping in Staged Columns",
          "content": "# Unit 6 assessment \u2014 Absorption and stripping\n\n## Problem 1: Solvent selection\n\n**Analytical prompt.** For Henry-law absorption, y*=m*x in dilute systems. Calculate or justify the consequence using the stated relation and a clearly declared consistent basis.\n\n**Numerical check.** At m=1.5 and x=0.02, y*=0.03 at equilibrium. Recreate this check, then change one input by 15% and calculate the sensitivity.\n\n**Conceptual discrimination.** Reactive absorption and nonideal solutions may violate the linear form.\n\n**Practical investigation.** Compare a simple equilibrium slope m with liquid-to-gas ratio and note how the absorption factor affects stage requirements.\n\n**Answer guidance.** State the governing equation and the numeric result from the given base case. The changed-input result depends on the parameter selected. A complete answer must include a physical-unit check and explicitly identify ignoring solvent losses and contaminant interactions can convert a seemingly efficient capture process into a costly waste-treatment problem.\n\n## Problem 2: Equilibrium stages for absorption\n\n**Analytical prompt.** Absorption factor A=L/(m*V). Calculate or justify the consequence using the stated relation and a clearly declared consistent basis.\n\n**Numerical check.** If L=180, V=100 and m=1.5 on consistent molar-flow basis, A=1.2. Recreate this check, then change one input by 15% and calculate the sensitivity.\n\n**Conceptual discrimination.** An absorption factor >1 does not guarantee arbitrary removal with finite stages.\n\n**Practical investigation.** Compare the absorber capacity at A below and above one using a transparent limiting analysis.\n\n**Answer guidance.** State the governing equation and the numeric result from the given base case. The changed-input result depends on the parameter selected. A complete answer must include a physical-unit check and explicitly identify a stage count based on equilibrium does not reveal tray efficiency, chemical reaction enhancement or hydraulic constraints.\n\n## Problem 3: Transfer-unit methods\n\n**Analytical prompt.** Ideal illustrative NTU relation C_out/C_in\u2248exp(-NTU). Calculate or justify the consequence using the stated relation and a clearly declared consistent basis.\n\n**Numerical check.** NTU=2.3 gives remaining fraction \u22480.1003, or about 90% removal. Recreate this check, then change one input by 15% and calculate the sensitivity.\n\n**Conceptual discrimination.** A real mass-transfer driving force may change sharply down the contactor.\n\n**Practical investigation.** Plot outlet concentration ratio against NTU and quantify diminishing returns at high separation demands.\n\n**Answer guidance.** State the governing equation and the numeric result from the given base case. The changed-input result depends on the parameter selected. A complete answer must include a physical-unit check and explicitly identify a constant ntu interpretation cannot be applied blindly when interfacial equilibrium or transfer coefficients vary strongly down the packed bed.\n\n## Problem 4: Solvent regeneration and stripping\n\n**Analytical prompt.** Solvent makeup balances purge, losses and degradation at steady inventory. Calculate or justify the consequence using the stated relation and a clearly declared consistent basis.\n\n**Numerical check.** If circulating solvent loses 0.4% per pass at 500 kg/h, makeup\u22482 kg/h before other losses. Recreate this check, then change one input by 15% and calculate the sensitivity.\n\n**Conceptual discrimination.** Solvent quality and chemical degradation demand separate tracking.\n\n**Practical investigation.** Construct a steady solvent-inventory balance including loss and purge, then explore the effect of regeneration efficiency.\n\n**Answer guidance.** State the governing equation and the numeric result from the given base case. The changed-input result depends on the parameter selected. A complete answer must include a physical-unit check and explicitly identify heating may degrade a solvent; reducing pressure may create foaming; steam use may increase wastewater treatment obligations."
